  "body": "https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/infrastructure.html#floating-point-numbers\n\n> A series of one or more ASCII digits.\n\nThe cited text appears to conflict with a subsequent part of the same section:\n\n> 1. Fraction: If the character indicated by position is a U+002E FULL STOP (.), run these substeps:\n>    1. Advance position to the next character.\n>    2. If position is past the end of input ... then jump to the step labeled conversion.\n\nAt that point, the currently-accumulated value is set to the integral portion of the number, and the \"conversion\" step deals with rounding the current value to something within range. There is nothing that says to return an error, NaN, empty string, or any other exceptional value.\n\nSo it seems that the spec is not entirely clear on whether or not digits can appear after the \".\".\n\nIf it's the case that the spec is clarified, I would suggest that it be done so in favor of supporting no digits after the \".\". This not only seems more generally useful, but is currently compatible with the way Safari and Chrome parse numbers in input[type=number] elements.\n",
